yes(801,'C','Simple monosaccharides such as glucose have the general formula (CH₂O)ₙ, giving C:H:O = 1:2:1. This ratio is not universal for every carbohydrate; polysaccharide formation removes water.',[ORG],question='For a simple monosaccharide represented by (CH₂O)ₙ, what is the C:H:O atom ratio?',options=['3:1:1','2:1:1','1:2:1','1:1:1'],audit='Restricted overgeneralised carbohydrate claim; removed merged pentane question.',check='carbohydrate-ratio')
yes(802,'A','Butanoate contributes CH₃CH₂CH₂COO–, written C₃H₇COO–. The ethyl group attached through oxygen is C₂H₅, so ethyl butanoate is C₃H₇COOC₂H₅.',[ORG],question='The condensed structural formula of ethyl butanoate is',options=['C₃H₇COOC₂H₅','C₂H₅COOC₃H₇','C₄H₉COOC₂H₅','C₂H₅COOC₄H₉'],audit='Corrected C→A: carbonyl carbon counts within four-carbon butanoate.',check='ester-carbon-count')
yes(803,'D','Benzene characteristically undergoes electrophilic substitution, replacing a ring hydrogen while restoring its aromatic electron system. Addition is possible under more forcing conditions but is not its characteristic reaction.',[ORG],question='Which reaction type is characteristic of benzene?',options=['Addition','Hydrolysis','Polymerisation','Substitution'])
